PROBE p02b.cob (PB60W02B containing PB60W02BI), --std 2023. Outer has `SPECIAL-NAMES. DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA.`; the contained program has no configuration section (it may not). Receivers PIC S9(9)V9(6), raw DISPLAY so the edited PICTURE cannot confound (trailing '{' = +0, '}' = -0).
LITERAL OUTPUT:
OUTER-COMMA-RAW=00000012345000{
OUTER-PERIOD-RAW=00000000000000{
INNER-COMMA-RAW=00000000000000{
INNER-PERIOD-RAW=00000012345000{
REACHED-END

EXIT=0

Outer: comma form = +123.450000, period form = 0 (correct, r5 honoured). Contained: comma form = 0 (REJECTED) and period form = +123.450000 (ACCEPTED) — the exact inversion of r5, with no diagnostic. An earlier run of p02.cob (PB60W02/PB60W02I) through edited receivers showed the same inversion (OUTER-COMMA=123,450000 / INNER-COMMA=0,000000).

PROBE p09.cob (PB60W09), --std 2023, `>>TURN EC-ARGUMENT-FUNCTION CHECKING ON`, receiver 01 R PIC S9(9)V9(6) (trailing overpunch '{' = +0, '}' = -0), 01 T PIC S9(4) ('D' = +4):
TN-MINMIN=000D
CTRL=00000001250000{
MINCR=00000001200000}
PLUSMIN=00000001200000}
MINMIN=00000001200000{
REACHED-END

EXIT=0

MINMIN: NUMVAL("-12-") = +12.000000 — POSITIVE, for a string that plainly contains a minus sign, with checking explicitly ON and NO exception (REACHED-END printed, exit 0). TN-MINMIN: the sibling TEST-NUMVAL("-12-") = +4, i.e. §15.93.4 reports character 4 IN ERROR for the same string. The two minus signs cancelled. Conforming carriers still behave (MINCR = -12, PLUSMIN "+12-" = -12, CTRL = +12.5).

BOTH HALVES STILL LIVE.
(1) UNANCHORED CURRENCY REMOVAL DESTROYS THE VALUE. PROBE p10e.cob (PB60W10E), --std 2023, checking OFF, receiver PIC S9(12)V99 ('{'=+0, 'N'=-5), T PIC S9(4):
TNC-C=000{
TNC-R=000{
TNC-D=000{
TNC-X=000{
NVC-C=0000000000000{
NVC-R=0000000000000{
NVC-D=0000000000000{
NVC-X=0000000001234N
REACHED-END

EXIT=0

TEST-NUMVAL-C returns 0 for all four pairs — by §15.94.4 1) a) the compiler's OWN validator certifies every one of them as CONFORMING. Yet NUMVAL-C("C123.45CR","C") = 0.00, NUMVAL-C("R123.45CR","R") = 0.00, NUMVAL-C("D123.45DB","D") = 0.00, while the non-colliding control NUMVAL-C("X123.45CR","X") = -123.45. This is a WRONG VALUE on input the compiler agrees is good, so §15.3's implementor-defined escape does not apply. With checking ON the same calls abort (see AR-15.68.3-4 evidence).
(2) THE RETURNED VALUE IS CHANNEL-DEPENDENT. PROBE p11.cob (PB60W11), --std 2023, WS-A = "$0.123456789", receiver 01 WS-R PIC S9(4)V9(9) ('I' = +9):
CMPT=000012345678I
MOVE=000012345600{
IFEQ=NO
IFTR=YES
RAW=0.123456
REACHED-END

EXIT=0

COMPUTE gives 0.123456789 (correct). `MOVE FUNCTION NUMVAL-C(WS-A) TO WS-R` gives 0.123456000. The relation condition agrees with the TRUNCATED value, not the true one (IFEQ=NO against 0.123456789, IFTR=YES against 0.123456000). A receiverless DISPLAY renders 0.123456. Four channels, three answers, one legal source program.

THE GROUPING OVER-BREADTH IS FIXED; THE CURRENCY OVER-BREADTH IS STILL LIVE.
FIXED — a grouping separator AFTER the decimal separator is no longer ignored. PROBE p06b.cob (PB60W06B), --std 2023, checking ON, argument-1 "1.2,3":
abnormal run-unit termination: EC-ARGUMENT-FUNCTION (fatal): NUMVAL-C argument-1 "1.2,3" does not conform to either §15.68.3 r4a format (first character in error at position 4; §15.94 TEST-NUMVAL-C reports the same position)

EXIT=1

(Position 4 is the comma. The note recorded this string silently valuing as 1.23.)
LIVE — the currency Replace still ignores something r2 never named. PROBE p10e.cob (PB60W10E), --std 2023, checking OFF:
TNC-C=000{
TNC-R=000{
TNC-D=000{
TNC-X=000{
NVC-C=0000000000000{
NVC-R=0000000000000{
NVC-D=0000000000000{
NVC-X=0000000001234N
REACHED-END

EXIT=0

With argument-2 = "C", NUMVAL-C("C123.45CR","C") = 0.00: the unanchored Replace deleted the C of the trailing "CR" — a character that is NOT the currency string, on argument-1 the compiler's own TEST-NUMVAL-C rates conforming (TNC-C=0). Expected -123.45. Control "X123.45CR"/"X" = -123.45. With checking ON, p10a.cob makes the mechanism explicit: `NUMVAL argument '123.45R' violates the §15.67.3 formats (unexpected character 'R')` — the residue after the over-broad Replace, verbatim.

THE NO-LOCALE SENTENCE STILL DIVERGES. PROBE p10e.cob (PB60W10E), --std 2023, checking OFF, receiver PIC S9(12)V99 (trailing '{' = +0, 'N' = -5):
TNC-C=000{
TNC-R=000{
TNC-D=000{
TNC-X=000{
NVC-C=0000000000000{
NVC-R=0000000000000{
NVC-D=0000000000000{
NVC-X=0000000001234N
REACHED-END

EXIT=0

NVC-R: NUMVAL-C("R123.45CR","R") = 0.00 — the string CONTAINS CR, TEST-NUMVAL-C rates it conforming (TNC-R=0), and the returned value is not negative. NVC-D: NUMVAL-C("D123.45DB","D") = 0.00, same shape. Control NVC-X = -123.45, so the sign convention itself works; the currency Replace runs FIRST and eats the R of "CR" (the D of "DB") before the EndsWith("CR"/"DB") scan can see it — the sign is not mis-signed, it is destroyed along with the value.
THE LOCALE SENTENCE REMAINS UNIMPLEMENTED. PROBE p12.cob (PB60W12), --std 2023, `FUNCTION NUMVAL-C(WS-A LOCALE)`:
  error COBOLNET1518: the LOCALE phrase of FUNCTION NUMVAL-C is in the optional locale module (ISO/IEC 1989:2023 Annex A §A.4.9), which COBOL.NET does not support — documented non-support, conformant per ISO §4.2.7 / §A.4.1. Use a supported alternative (e.g. STANDARD-1/STANDARD-2 collating, FORMATTED-DATE/-TIME, or NUMVAL-C without the LOCALE phrase).

EXIT=70

(The LOCALE half now carries an explicit §4.2.7 optional-module disposition — an owner-level question, not the live defect. The live defect is the no-LOCALE sentence.)
